{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f7c239109f0a7e3", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# What Matters for Diffusion-Friendly Latent Manifold? \\\\ Prior-Aligned Autoencoders for Latent Diffusion\narXiv: 2605.07915\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Identification of three key properties of a diffusion-friendly latent manifold: coherent spatial structure, local manifold continuity, and global manifold semantics.\n  2. Demonstration that these three manifold properties are more consistent with downstream generation quality than reconstruction fidelity alone.\n  3. Proposal of the Prior-Aligned Autoencoder (PAE) framework that explicitly shapes the latent manifold instead of leaving it to emerge indirectly from reconstruction.\n  4. Introduction of Spatial Structure Regularization (SSR) to enhance instance-level spatial structure by aligning latents with refined VFM features.\n  5. Introduction of Manifold Continuity Regularization (MCR) to promote local manifold continuity through cascaded perturbation-based perceptual consistency.\n  6. Introduction of Semantic Consistency Regularization (SCR) to preserve global manifold semantics by aligning latents with globally pooled VFM features.\n  7. Development of a VFM prior refinement strategy that compresses and spatially refines frozen VFM features into better bottleneck-matched alignment targets.\n  8. Achievement of state-of-the-art generation quality on ImageNet 256x256 with a gFID of 1.03 and up to 13x faster convergence compared to existing strong tokenizers.\n---\n# Self-Supervised Flow Matching for Scalable Multi-Modal Synthesis\narXiv: 2603.06507\nAuthors: Hila Chefer, Patrick Esser, Dominik Lorenz, Dustin Podell, Vikash Raja, Vinh Tong, Antonio Torralba, Robin Rombach\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of Self-Flow, a self-supervised flow matching paradigm that integrates representation learning within the generative framework without external supervision.\n  2. Proposal of Dual-Timestep Scheduling, which applies heterogeneous noise levels across tokens to create information asymmetry that forces the model to learn strong semantic representations.\n  3. Demonstration that external alignment methods exhibit unexpected scaling behavior and fail to generalize across modalities, often degrading performance in video and audio generation.\n  4. Empirical validation that Self-Flow outperforms leading external alignment methods like REPA on image, video, and audio generation tasks while following expected scaling laws.\n  5. Proof-of-concept that the self-supervised framework naturally extends to joint multi-modal training and embodied AI tasks, yielding consistent improvements across all modalities.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "df561d9f-1773-456b-9343-9527c8ef667b", "uuid": "e4ece85a-5b2a-4977-bad8-361b972dcc75", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T09:25:05.254Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# What Matters for Diffusion-Friendly Latent Manifold? \\\\ Prior-Aligned Autoencoders for Latent Diffusion\narXiv: 2605.07915\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Identification of three key properties of a diffusion-friendly latent manifold: coherent spatial structure, local manifold continuity, and global manifold semantics.\n  2. Demonstration that these three manifold properties are more consistent with downstream generation quality than reconstruction fidelity alone.\n  3. Proposal of the Prior-Aligned Autoencoder (PAE) framework that explicitly shapes the latent manifold instead of leaving it to emerge indirectly from reconstruction.\n  4. Introduction of Spatial Structure Regularization (SSR) to enhance instance-level spatial structure by aligning latents with refined VFM features.\n  5. Introduction of Manifold Continuity Regularization (MCR) to promote local manifold continuity through cascaded perturbation-based perceptual consistency.\n  6. Introduction of Semantic Consistency Regularization (SCR) to preserve global manifold semantics by aligning latents with globally pooled VFM features.\n  7. Development of a VFM prior refinement strategy that compresses and spatially refines frozen VFM features into better bottleneck-matched alignment targets.\n  8. Achievement of state-of-the-art generation quality on ImageNet 256x256 with a gFID of 1.03 and up to 13x faster convergence compared to existing strong tokenizers.\n---\n# Self-Supervised Flow Matching for Scalable Multi-Modal Synthesis\narXiv: 2603.06507\nAuthors: Hila Chefer, Patrick Esser, Dominik Lorenz, Dustin Podell, Vikash Raja, Vinh Tong, Antonio Torralba, Robin Rombach\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of Self-Flow, a self-supervised flow matching paradigm that integrates representation learning within the generative framework without external supervision.\n  2. Proposal of Dual-Timestep Scheduling, which applies heterogeneous noise levels across tokens to create information asymmetry that forces the model to learn strong semantic representations.\n  3. Demonstration that external alignment methods exhibit unexpected scaling behavior and fail to generalize across modalities, often degrading performance in video and audio generation.\n  4. Empirical validation that Self-Flow outperforms leading external alignment methods like REPA on image, video, and audio generation tasks while following expected scaling laws.\n  5. Proof-of-concept that the self-supervised framework naturally extends to joint multi-modal training and embodied AI tasks, yielding consistent improvements across all modalities.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
